  "index": 8,
  "response": "```python\ndef bubbleSort(a):\n    for i in range(len(a) - 1):\n        for j in range(len(a) - i - 1):\n            if a[j] > a[j + 1]:\n                a[j], a[j + 1] = a[j + 1], a[j]\n    return a\n\n\n# Driver code\na = [str(i) for i in range(500)]  # generate an array of 500 strings\nbubbleSort(a)\nprint(a)\n```\n\n**Explanation:**\n\n* **Bubble Sort** is a simple sorting algorithm that repeatedly compares adjacent elements in an array. If they are out of order, they are swapped. \n* The algorithm starts from the first element and works its way through the array until it reaches the end. \n* It repeats this process until the array is sorted.\n* **Driver Code:**\n    * We generate an array of 500 strings using the `[str(i) for i in range(500)]` list comprehension.\n    * We call the `bubbleSort()` function to sort the array.\n    * We print the sorted array.\n\n**Output:**\n\nThe sorted array will be a list of 500 strings, sorted alphabetically.\n\n**Time Complexity:**\n\n* Worst and average case: O(n^2)\n* Best case: O(n)\n\n**Space Complexity:**\n\n* O(1)\n\n**Note:**\n\n* Bubble Sort is a simple sorting algorithm, but it is not suitable for large datasets. More efficient sorting algorithms exist for such scenarios.\n* The sorting criterion in this code is alphabetical order. You can customize the sorting criterion as needed.",
